talk to me about whether schools should  be going back on the 1st of june  well i think schools should be going  back if it's safe and  one of the things that schools are  grappling with at the moment is whether  they can  safely reopen and comply with things  like social distancing rules so  some primary schools in my constituency  are going to go back on the 1st of june  but quite a lot aren't and it just  depends a lot on practical factors some  of the schools are  bigger they've got more outside space so  they've been able to put in measures to  do it  others are still trying to work with the  local authority to find a way  to do it my own child's school probably  isn't going to go back on the 1st of  june but they're hoping to be up and  running  a couple of within a couple of weeks  after that so it's important to get  children back into school of course it  is and  you know i'm like every family i want to  see my child back in school but it's got  to be safe and they've got to be able to  do it  right do you think it's safe at the  moment um  i think it can be i mean it just depends  very much on local  circumstances one of the things that  teachers  tell me that they're really worried  about is the fact that there isn't any  national guidance around it so  at the moment you've got a situation  across the country where  head teachers are trying to make  judgments um  often with the support of local  authorities which is very helpful  my own council has been really really  helpful and supportive to schools in  trying to make sure that they get those  measures in place  and but even the government has accepted  now that  for some schools this is going to take  longer and just giving a few weeks  notice probably wasn't the right way to  go about it  uh we're chatting to a gp uh from north  west london a second ago and he was  saying that he saw an increase  yesterday at uh surgeries after the bank  holiday weekend he doesn't know whether  that  is um because of uh blurring  of the boundaries when it comes to  lockdown rules given what happened with  dominic cummings and he doesn't know  whether  that could result in a second spike if  it does is it dominic dominic cummings  and the prime minister's fault  well i mean i don't i probably like him  i don't know why he's he's had more  people in his surgery but i think there  is a really  really big moment coming because we're  easing out of lockdown that  that requires a series of political  judgments as well as scientific  judgments and people need to understand  the basis of the advice that they're  being given and they need to trust it my  big worry about what's happened over the  weekend  is that i think increasingly people will  feel that they can't trust the guidance  that the government is putting out and  it's one of the reasons why  labour hasn't called for dominic  cummings to be sacked the prime minister  said he's not going to sack him  but we've said if he isn't going to be  sacked then the prime minister must  clear up a number of outstanding issues  about how it was that his  own advisor appeared to breach  repeatedly the guidance that he'd  written  otherwise we're not going to see public  confidence restored and my big  fear is that people will start to just  use their own  instincts as dominic cummings said he  did himself and  that will leave the country in a very um  bad place  we've heard from the prime minister he  says that he his advisor didn't break  the rules we had a very lengthy news  conference in the rose garden  of downing street end of don't we need  to move on  now and talk instead about how we get  our country out of lockdown  yeah i mean i think we do we haven't  just got the public health crisis that  we're currently dealing with but we've  also  got a huge problem in relation to  what we do now to try and get the  economy back on track just in the last  few days  um a major company you probably know k  shearings in wigan  went into administration hundreds of job  losses here thousands around the country  and and some of those employees have  worked for the company  for 25 years this is quite devastating  so we do need to move on  but we need to move on in a way that the  public can have confidence about the  advice that they're being given and the  decisions that are being taken  and if there's one rule for government  advisors and one rule for the rest of us  people will not have confidence and they  won't follow the guidance so  the prime minister urgently needs to  clarify why it is that he believes that  dominic cummings didn't break those  rules  okay so shearing's the is the coach  company yeah yeah it's a it's a holiday  travel company so  it's a slg so travel accommodation  and and the coach company's sharings  yeah but it's a  long-standing company um that was a  perfectly healthy profitable business  that went out of business because of  kovid19 and we're seeing this  right across the country okay let me ask  you a couple of questions  uh pertaining to your brief if i may and  nazarene sagari ratcliffe first  of all we are all hoping against hope  for good news uh today and that she will  be  pardoned um have you managed to speak to  dominic rob about any idea  yeah so i've reached out to the family  and to and to the  to ministers as well i'm really  concerned about the prospects that  nazanin might be returned to prison one  of the things that we  alongside the family have been urging  dominic robb to do  is to send the uk ambassador to visit  nazanin  as a show of solidarity because she was  granted diplomatic immunity  and it's really important that in a  moment of such distress that britain  stands up  and shows that it will stand up for for  families like  the nazanin and her her young child and  husband  um i i the family are understandably  very very worried that if she's returned  to prison that that could signal a very  lengthy  spell again in prison and after  everything this family's been through i  think we desperately need to see  um a resolution to this situation and  nazanin brought home and reunited with  her family  okay and you've also written to dominic  robert believe about the situation in  hong kong  what do you want to see happen there  well first of all i think the government  needs to  set out some concrete measures that it  wants both the hong kong  authorities and china to take an  investigation into  police brutality an independent  investigation is really really important  you'll have seen  the scenes from hong kong in recent days  the use of tear gas and pepper spray  against pro-democracy protesters we have  a  special and long-standing relationship  with the people of hong kong  and we've got to stand up now especially  because this isn't the first time  that we've seen china trying to erode  the provisions set out in the joint  declaration that we signed  several years ago these are the things  that protect rights freedoms democracy  and the rule of law and these are these  values are the basis of our  international alliances  around the world we've seen with kovid  19 how important those alliances are we  cannot afford to let them be eroded now  um and just before we let you go um i  won't go if i want to go to spain  this summer am i going to have to take  three weeks leave for a week's holiday  it's not at all clear the government  seven times has  announced quarantine measures and then  rode back on them in the last  couple of um couple of days they've said  that they want to introduce quarantine  measures where people  will have to self-isolate for two weeks  after they return from most countries  overseas  in the absence of any other measures  that seems to me to be a sensible  temporary stop gap but actually what we  need is a proper system  of test track and trace up and running  if people were tested  when they returned from overseas or even  better still  before they got on the airplane then we  wouldn't be 